;; Passing an ordinary function where a bjoroutine is expected.
;;
;; The other half of `bjo_as_value.bjo`, and for a long time it was reported as
;; that one: `unifyEffect` had a single message for both directions, so this
;; said "a bjoroutine cannot be used where an ordinary function is expected"
;; about a program containing no bjoroutine at all.
;;
;; It is also the direction with something to explain. `defun` is
;; colour-polymorphic, so the reader has every reason to expect this to work —
;; and it does, for a `defun` that can suspend. `pure-work` cannot: nothing in
;; its call graph reaches a leaf with two bodies, so no second copy was
;; generated and there is no second colour of it to pass. That is the sentence
;; the message owes them, and it is why this fixture exists separately.

;; EXPECT-ERROR: an ordinary function cannot be used where a bjoroutine is expected
;; EXPECT-ERROR: A defun is colour-polymorphic only as far as it can suspend
;; EXPECT-ERROR: Define it with defbjo if it should suspend

(: pure-work (-> int int))
(defun (pure-work x) (* x 2))

(: apply-bjo (-> (-bjo-> int int) int int))
(defbjo (apply-bjo f x) (f x))

(: use-it (-bjo-> int int))
(defbjo (use-it n) (apply-bjo pure-work n))

(defun (main args) 0)
